#: ../../../aliases.rst:377
msgid "``source-foreign``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:378
msgid ""
"Like the ``source`` command but for files in foreign (non-xonsh) languages. "
"It will pick up the environment and any aliases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:381
msgid ""
"Supported shells: ``bash``, ``zsh``, ``sh`` (also ``dash``, ``ash``, "
"``ksh``, ``mksh``, ``pdksh``), and ``cmd`` on Windows. Absolute paths like "
"``/bin/bash`` or ``/bin/sh`` are canonicalised to the same defaults."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:386
msgid ""
"The convenience wrappers below pre-set the ``shell`` and ``--sourcer`` "
"arguments for the most common cases — every flag accepted by ``source-"
"foreign`` is available on them as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:1
msgid ""
"usage: source-foreign [-h] [-i] [-l] [--envcmd ENVCMD] [--aliascmd "
"ALIASCMD]\n"
"                      [--extra-args EXTRA_ARGS] [-u] [-p PREVCMD]\n"
"                      [--postcmd POSTCMD] [--funcscmd FUNCSCMD]\n"
"                      [--sourcer SOURCER] [--use-tmpfile]\n"
"                      [--seterrprevcmd SETERRPREVCMD]\n"
"                      [--seterrpostcmd SETERRPOSTCMD] [--overwrite-aliases]\n"
"                      [--suppress-skip-message] [--show] [--show-output] [-"
"d]\n"
"                      [-n]\n"
"                      shell files_or_code [files_or_code ...]\n"
"\n"
"Sources a file written in a foreign shell language.\n"
"\n"
"positional arguments:\n"
"  shell                 Name or path to the foreign shell\n"
"  files_or_code         file paths to source or code in the target "
"language.\n"
"\n"
"options:\n"
"  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n"
"  -i, --interactive     whether the sourced shell should be interactive\n"
"  -l, --login           whether the sourced shell should be login\n"
"  --envcmd ENVCMD       command to print environment\n"
"  --aliascmd ALIASCMD   command to print aliases\n"
"  --extra-args EXTRA_ARGS\n"
"                        extra arguments needed to run the shell\n"
"  -u, --unsafe          whether the source shell should be run safely, and "
"not raise any errors, even if they occur.\n"
"  -p, --prevcmd PREVCMD\n"
"                        command(s) to run before any other commands, "
"replaces traditional source.\n"
"  --postcmd POSTCMD     command(s) to run after all other commands\n"
"  --funcscmd FUNCSCMD   code to find locations of all native functions in "
"the shell language.\n"
"  --sourcer SOURCER     the source command in the target shell language.\n"
"                        If this is not set, a default value will attempt to "
"be\n"
"                        looked up based on the shell name.\n"
"  --use-tmpfile         whether the commands for source shell should be "
"written to a temporary file.\n"
"  --seterrprevcmd SETERRPREVCMD\n"
"                        command(s) to set exit-on-error before any other "
"commands.\n"
"  --seterrpostcmd SETERRPOSTCMD\n"
"                        command(s) to set exit-on-error after all other "
"commands.\n"
"  --overwrite-aliases   flag for whether or not sourced aliases should "
"replace the current xonsh aliases.\n"
"  --suppress-skip-message\n"
"                        flag for whether or not skip messages should be "
"suppressed.\n"
"  --show                show the generated shell command that will be sent "
"to the foreign\n"
"                        shell (does not show what the sourced script prints "
"— see\n"
"                        ``--show-output`` for that).\n"
"  --show-output         forward stdout and stderr produced by the sourced "
"script to the\n"
"                        xonsh terminal. By default they are silently "
"discarded.\n"
"  -d, --dry-run         Will not actually source the file.\n"
"  -n, --non-interactive\n"
"                        Deprecated: The default mode runs in non-interactive "
"mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:394
msgid "``source-sh``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:395
msgid ""
"Thin wrapper around ``source-foreign sh`` with ``--sourcer .`` (the POSIX "
"dot builtin, since ``/bin/sh`` on dash-based distros does not understand "
"``source``). Use this to source ``/etc/profile`` and similar POSIX "
"configuration files. Also covers ``dash``, ``ash``, ``ksh``, ``mksh``, and "
"``pdksh`` (env and aliases are picked up; shell-specific function listing is "
"not)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:404
msgid "``source-bash``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:405
msgid "Thin wrapper around ``source-foreign bash`` with ``--sourcer source``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:409
msgid "``source-zsh``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:410
msgid "Thin wrapper around ``source-foreign zsh`` with ``--sourcer source``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:414
msgid "Windows Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:417
msgid "cmd-based Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:418
msgid ""
"The following aliases on Windows are expanded to ``['cmd', '/c', alias]``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:420
msgid ""
"{'cls': ['cmd', '/c', 'cls'],\n"
" 'copy': ['cmd', '/c', 'copy'],\n"
" 'del': ['cmd', '/c', 'del'],\n"
" 'dir': ['cmd', '/c', 'dir'],\n"
" 'erase': ['cmd', '/c', 'erase'],\n"
" 'md': ['cmd', '/c', 'md'],\n"
" 'mkdir': ['cmd', '/c', 'mkdir'],\n"
" 'mklink': ['cmd', '/c', 'mklink'],\n"
" 'move': ['cmd', '/c', 'move'],\n"
" 'rd': ['cmd', '/c', 'rd'],\n"
" 'ren': ['cmd', '/c', 'ren'],\n"
" 'rename': ['cmd', '/c', 'rename'],\n"
" 'rmdir': ['cmd', '/c', 'rmdir'],\n"
" 'time': ['cmd', '/c', 'time'],\n"
" 'type': ['cmd', '/c', 'type'],\n"
" 'vol': ['cmd', '/c', 'vol'],\n"
" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:443
msgid "``activate``/``deactivate`` on Windows with Anaconda"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:444
msgid ""
"On Windows with an Anaconda Python distribution, ``activate`` and "
"``deactivate`` are aliased to ``['source-cmd', 'activate.bat']`` and "
"``['source-cmd', 'deactivate.bat']``. This makes it possible to use the same "
"commands to activate/deactivate conda environments as in cmd.exe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:451
msgid "``sudo`` on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:452
msgid ""
"On Windows, if no executables named ``sudo`` are found, Xonsh adds a "
"``sudo`` alias that poly fills the \"run as Admin\" behavior with the help "
"of ``ShellExecuteEx`` and ``ctypes``. It doesn't support any actual ``sudo`` "
"parameters and just takes the command to run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:459
msgid "User Aliases with Descriptions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:461
msgid ""
"Every alias can carry a one-line description. It surfaces in the tab-"
"completion dropdown next to the alias name, and in the ``cmd?`` / ``cmd??`` "
"help output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:464
msgid "For callable aliases, the function's docstring is used automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:466
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register('qwe')\n"
"  def _qwe():\n"
"      \"\"\"List files in long format.\"\"\"\n"
"      ls -la\n"
"\n"
"@ qw<TAB>\n"
"qwe  List files in long format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:476
msgid ""
"For string and list aliases — which have nowhere to attach a docstring — use "
"the dict form:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:479
msgid ""
"@ aliases['qwe'] = {'alias': 'ls -la', 'doc': 'List files'}\n"
"@ aliases |= {\n"
"      'psg':  {'alias': ['ps', 'aux'], 'doc': 'Process list'},\n"
"      'g':    {'alias': 'git',         'doc': 'Git wrapper'},\n"
"  }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:487
msgid ""
"The dict form recognises two keys: ``'alias'`` (required, the alias value "
"itself — string, list, or callable) and ``'doc'`` (optional, the one-line "
"description). Other keys are reserved for future use and currently ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:491
msgid ""
"When ``'doc'`` is set on a callable alias, it overrides the function's own "
"``__doc__`` — handy for showing a shorter summary in completions while "
"keeping a longer docstring in the source:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:495
msgid ""
"@ def _bar():\n"
"      \"\"\"Long, detailed description of bar...\"\"\"\n"
"      echo bar\n"
"@ aliases['bar'] = {'alias': _bar, 'doc': 'Short summary'}\n"
"@ ba<TAB>\n"
"bar  Short summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:504
msgid ""
"Reassigning an alias without a ``'doc'`` clears the previous description, so "
"descriptions never accidentally stick to a different value bound under the "
"same name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../aliases.rst:508
msgid ""
"By default, the description shows in the dropdown only when the alias has a "
"docstring (or an explicit ``'doc'``). To also show the binary path for non-"
"alias commands, set ``$CMD_COMPLETIONS_SHOW_DESC = True``."
msgstr ""

